    )
What is the best redesign?

Your answer is correct
A. Filter Orders in the gallery

Explanation
This choice suggests filtering the orders directly in the gallery, which can improve the search experience by reducing the number of items displayed based on the search criteria. This approach can make the search more responsive and efficient by only showing relevant orders.
B. Increase row limit to 2000

Explanation
Increasing the row limit to 2000 may not necessarily improve the responsiveness of the order search experience. While it can potentially display more orders in the gallery, it may not address the responsiveness issue caused by the current formula design.
C. Collect inside a ForAll loop

Explanation
Collecting inside a ForAll loop may not be the best redesign for improving the order search experience. Using a ForAll loop to collect data can introduce performance issues, especially when dealing with large datasets. It may not address the responsiveness requirement effectively.
D. SaveData the browser cache

Explanation
Saving data to the browser cache using SaveData may not directly impact the responsiveness of the order search experience. While caching data can improve performance in certain scenarios, it may not be the most effective solution for optimizing the search functionality in this context.
Overall explanation
The best redesign is to filter the remote Orders source directly in GalleryOrders.Items instead of first copying the full table into colOrders. Microsoft notes that direct gallery binding to a remote data source keeps payloads small through paging, and delegation lets the source do the filtering work. That is both faster and more scalable than loading the full table up front and then searching locally.



This snippet is also inefficient because it performs a large ClearCollect during App.OnStart, which Microsoft identifies as a common cause of slow app startup. A search experience should generally evaluate the query when needed, not force the app to preload the entire transactional table before the first screen becomes useful. Moving the filter to the gallery keeps startup lighter and preserves delegation opportunities.



Why the other options are incorrect:

• B. Increasing the row limit to 2000 does not fix the underlying design problem. Microsoft explains that raising the limit can still leave you with partial results for nondelegable scenarios and can worsen performance. The app would still preload far more data than necessary and still rely on client-side filtering over a collection. This option changes a threshold, not the architecture.

• C. Collecting inside a ForAll loop would usually make the design worse, not better. Microsoft guidance on query optimization warns against app-side patterns that generate many calls and repeated work, because they slow the app and increase network traffic. This option adds complexity without restoring delegation or reducing startup work. It is an anti-pattern for a large-table search screen.

• D. SaveData is not the right answer here because the problem is not “how do I cache a correct local result,” but “how do I avoid collecting a large table in the first place.” Microsoft also notes that SaveData has browser limitations and depends on memory-constrained in-memory collections. Even where it is appropriate, it is used for caching or offline support, not as a replacement for a delegable live-search design over a large table. This makes it a plausible but still incorrect distractor.

