This is also the lowest-risk change because it reduces payload size without changing the logical result set. The query guidance further notes that $top and paging control row counts, but column trimming through $select is the direct answer when the issue is oversized response bodies caused by unnecessary properties.



Why the other options are incorrect:

• A. Prefer: return=representation is used mainly for create or update operations when the caller wants returned entity data. Microsoft documents it in the context of POST and PATCH, not as a retrieval optimization for GET. It would not address a problem caused by returning too many columns on a query.

• B. POST is not the standard method for retrieving rows in the Dataverse Web API. Microsoft states that GET is used for retrieve operations, while POST is used for create operations or actions. Changing the method would alter the operation type instead of improving the payload of the current retrieval.

• D. $batch can consolidate multiple requests into one HTTP call, but it does not by itself reduce the columns returned by an individual query. Microsoft describes batch as a grouping mechanism, not as a substitute for column projection. If the oversized response comes from one retrieval returning too many properties, $select is the correct first change.

